# The gun's shot, and the one thing on the field that is not in `actors.crpix`.
#
# 8 x 8 texels at `art::TEXELS_PER_UNIT` = 20 is 0.4 x 0.4 world units, against
# a `game::BOLT_RADIUS` of 0.15 — **deliberately larger than what it sweeps**,
# as asteroids' shot is and for the same reason. The radius is the width of the
# swept sphere the CCD uses, not a collider anything can be hit *by*, so a shot
# drawn at 0.3 units would be six texels of dust for no gain.
#
# # Why this is a second sheet
#
# Every other picture in this game shares one 34-texel frame so the whole field
# is one batch (see `actors.crpix`). A shot is 8 texels; putting it in that
# sheet would draw a 0.4-unit picture inside a 1.7-unit quad — twenty times the
# fill, on the one thing that is fired four times a second and lives 0.6 of one.
# Two sheets is two batches a frame and both numbers are constant in the size of
# the horde, which is what the scale sub-slice is measuring.
#
# A hot core in a warm rim, which is what makes it read as a tracer rather than
# as a square.

crpix: 8 8 1 3 1

palette:
  . c None
  o c #ffb347
  w c #fff6d5

frame shot:
  ..oooo..
  .oooooo.
  oowwwwoo
  oowwwwoo
  oowwwwoo
  oowwwwoo
  .oooooo.
  ..oooo..

sample: pixel
